Here's a hunt pack for T1053.005, keyed to your indexes.

## 1. Baseline — all task creation

```spl
index=winevents (EventCode=4698 OR EventCode=4699 
  OR EventCode=4700 OR EventCode=4701 OR EventCode=4702)
| eval action=case(EventCode=4698,"Created",EventCode=4699,"Deleted",
    EventCode=4700,"Enabled",EventCode=4701,"Disabled",EventCode=4702,"Updated")
| stats count values(action) as actions values(Account_Name) as creator 
    dc(ComputerName) as host_count values(ComputerName) as hosts by TaskName
| sort + host_count
```
Low `host_count` = your candidates. Anything on 1–2 hosts deserves a look.

## 2. Suspicious content in the task XML (4698)

```spl
index=winevents EventCode=4698
| rex field=TaskContent "<Command>(?<task_cmd>[^<]+)</Command>"
| rex field=TaskContent "<Arguments>(?<task_args>[^<]+)</Arguments>"
| eval full=task_cmd." ".task_args
| search full IN ("*powershell*","*cmd.exe*","*rundll32*","*mshta*",
    "*wscript*","*cscript*","*regsvr32*","*certutil*","*curl*","*bitsadmin*")
    OR full IN ("*-enc*","*-w hidden*","*FromBase64*","*DownloadString*",
    "*\\Temp\\*","*\\AppData\\*","*\\Public\\*","*\\ProgramData\\*")
| table _time ComputerName Account_Name TaskName task_cmd task_args
```

## 3. Create → delete churn (anti-forensics / one-shot tasks)

```spl
index=winevents (EventCode=4698 OR EventCode=4699)
| stats min(_time) as first max(_time) as last 
    values(EventCode) as codes by TaskName, ComputerName
| where mvcount(codes)=2
| eval lifespan_min=round((last-first)/60,1)
| where lifespan_min < 60
| convert ctime(first) ctime(last)
```
APT34 tradecraft fits here — tasks that exist just long enough to run.

## 4. Remote task creation

```spl
index=winevents EventCode=4698
| join type=left ComputerName 
    [ search index=winevents EventCode=4624 Logon_Type=3 
      | fields ComputerName, Account_Name, Source_Network_Address, _time
      | rename Account_Name as net_account ]
| where isnotnull(Source_Network_Address) 
    AND Source_Network_Address!="-" AND Source_Network_Address!="127.0.0.1"
| table _time ComputerName TaskName Account_Name net_account Source_Network_Address
```
Tighten the join window with `| where abs(_time - orig_time) < 300` if it's noisy.

## 5. Sysmon — execution under the scheduler

```spl
index=winevents sourcetype=*Sysmon* EventCode=1
  (ParentImage="*\\svchost.exe*" OR ParentImage="*\\taskeng.exe*" 
   OR ParentImage="*\\taskhostw.exe*")
| search Image IN ("*\\powershell.exe","*\\cmd.exe","*\\wscript.exe",
    "*\\cscript.exe","*\\mshta.exe","*\\rundll32.exe","*\\regsvr32.exe")
| stats count values(CommandLine) as cmds by Computer, User, Image
| sort - count
```

Plus the on-disk artifacts, which catch tasks registered outside the API:

```spl
index=winevents sourcetype=*Sysmon* (EventCode=11 OR EventCode=13)
  (TargetFilename="C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\*" 
   OR TargetObject="*\\CurrentVersion\\Schedule\\TaskCache\\Tree\\*")
| table _time Computer Image TargetFilename TargetObject User
```

## 6. schtasks.exe / at.exe invocation (covers .002)

```spl
index=winevents (EventCode=4688 OR (sourcetype=*Sysmon* EventCode=1))
  (Image="*\\schtasks.exe" OR Image="*\\at.exe" 
   OR New_Process_Name="*\\schtasks.exe" OR New_Process_Name="*\\at.exe")
| eval cmd=coalesce(CommandLine, Process_Command_Line)
| search cmd IN ("*/create*","*/change*","*/ru system*","*/s *","*/xml*")
| table _time ComputerName Account_Name cmd
```

## 7. CrowdStrike FDR

```spl
index=crowdstrike event_simpleName IN 
    (ScheduledTaskRegistered, ScheduledTaskModified, ScheduledTaskDeleted)
| stats count dc(aid) as host_count values(TaskExecCommand) as cmd 
    values(TaskAuthor) as author by TaskName, event_simpleName
| where host_count <= 3
```

## 8. Linux side — cron (.003) and systemd timers (.006)

```spl
index=os (sourcetype=linux_secure OR sourcetype=cron OR sourcetype=syslog)
  ("crontab" OR "CRON" OR "systemd-run" OR "systemctl" ".timer")
| search NOT (user IN ("root","backup") AND "run-parts")
| table _time host user process message
```

---

**Two things to do before you hunt.** First, confirm your data actually covers this — run `index=winevents EventCode=4698 | stats dc(ComputerName)` against your known endpoint count. 4698 requires the "Other Object Access Events" subcategory, and it's off by default in a lot of GPO baselines. If coverage is thin, that's your Visibility column answer (Partial/No), and Sysmon + CrowdStrike carry the weight.

Second, build a 30–90 day allowlist of known-good task names before running #1 and #7, otherwise Google Update, Dell/HP OEM tasks, and your patch agent will bury everything.